WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:21.900
Narrator: This is
Charlie Manx, the villain

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.780
in AMC's new series "NOS4A2."

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:28.130
He's played by sometimes-bald
actor Zachary Quinto

00:00:28.130 --> 00:00:29.890
and was designed by this guy,

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:32.580
Oscar-winning FX artist Joel Harlow.

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.310
Joel's work has been seen in
tons of great movies like...

00:00:35.310 --> 00:00:37.680
- All five of the "Pirates
of the Caribbean" films,

00:00:37.680 --> 00:00:41.228
the first J.J. Abrams
"Star Trek," "Lone Ranger,"

00:00:41.228 --> 00:00:44.503
"Logan," the new
"Hellboy," "Black Panther."

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:48.420
- I visited his workspace to learn

00:00:48.420 --> 00:00:50.360
how he brought Charlie Manx to life,

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.010
and we looked at some of
his other amazing creations.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:57.180
Yo, I'm the new Hellboy!

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.223
Joel: There you go.

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:08.650
Joel: I was approached
by production about doing

00:01:08.650 --> 00:01:12.480
a transformational age
makeup on Zach Quinto,

00:01:12.480 --> 00:01:15.160
who I've worked with on
several films, obviously,

00:01:15.160 --> 00:01:16.410
both "Star Trek" films.

00:01:16.410 --> 00:01:18.800
Narrator: In "NOS4A2,"
Charlie Manx is a supernatural

00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:21.020
creature who feeds off
the souls of children,

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.090
kidnapping them in a car called The Wraith

00:01:23.090 --> 00:01:27.650
with the vanity plate
NOS4A2, or Nosferatu.

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:30.810
It's based on a novel of the
same name by author Joe Hill,

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.970
son of award-winning author Tabitha King

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:34.490
and some guy named Steve.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:38.660
- What you see here is
a silicone head of Zach.

00:01:38.660 --> 00:01:40.730
Once the makeup is sculpted,
you know what you have.

00:01:40.730 --> 00:01:42.610
Let's just say we're talking
about the 100-year-old.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:44.385
Once the entire makeup is sculpted

00:01:44.385 --> 00:01:46.335
on the stone copy of Zach's head,

00:01:46.335 --> 00:01:49.240
then you decide where you're
gonna cut the makeup apart.

00:01:49.240 --> 00:01:52.420
Cut the neck off, we'll cut
the chin and lower lip off,

00:01:52.420 --> 00:01:54.190
we'll cut the upper lip and nose off,

00:01:54.190 --> 00:01:57.520
cheeks, forehead, earlobes,
and then when you're dealing

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:59.650
with a 135-year-old,
there's the back of the head

00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:01.620
because his hair at that point is so thin

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:04.170
that you see through
the hair to the scalp.

00:02:04.170 --> 00:02:06.100
Narrator: Creating a
character from a popular book

00:02:06.100 --> 00:02:07.310
is no easy task.

00:02:07.310 --> 00:02:09.550
Luckily for Joel, he knew the author.

00:02:09.550 --> 00:02:12.150
They met in 1994 when both were working

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.130
on the TV mini-series "The Stand."

00:02:14.130 --> 00:02:17.850
- Well, fortunately, I
am friends with Joe Hill.

00:02:17.850 --> 00:02:20.370
He's the go-to guy. I
mean, he created Manx.

00:02:20.370 --> 00:02:23.690
One of his biggest notes
to me was Manx's teeth,

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:26.450
even when he's in his earlier stages,

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.140
are still very sorta desiccated.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:32.250
It's like 1800s dental work.

00:02:32.250 --> 00:02:36.079
- How much does, when
designing this, did Zach's

00:02:36.079 --> 00:02:41.079
actual character of his face
play into making the character?

00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:45.650
- Oh, it played into his
character right off the bat.

00:02:45.650 --> 00:02:49.180
You know, and I never
like to design a makeup

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:52.620
independently of the actor or actress

00:02:52.620 --> 00:02:53.960
that is playing that character.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:55.660
It's always very collaborative.

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:59.320
So there's a lot of, a
lot of restraint required

00:02:59.320 --> 00:03:00.340
when you're doing something like that

00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:03.520
as opposed to doing a movie
like "Star Trek Beyond"

00:03:03.520 --> 00:03:06.200
where you're creating
aliens that nobody's seen.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:08.270
Got a lot of leeway there.

00:03:08.270 --> 00:03:10.050
Hellboy, you got a lot of leeway there.

00:03:10.050 --> 00:03:12.420
It's not a character
that you see at the store

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:14.700
unless you're shopping
somewhere very unusual.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:15.580
- It'd be weird if you keep

00:03:15.580 --> 00:03:17.300
running into Hellboy in your normal life.

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:20.354
- It's a lot of just
dialing it in so it looks

00:03:20.354 --> 00:03:24.680
like a believable old-age character,

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:29.330
yet maintains the characteristics
of, in this case, Zach.

00:03:29.330 --> 00:03:33.290
I would text Zach images
of the designs, you know,

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:35.970
and get his sign-off,
so he was very involved

00:03:35.970 --> 00:03:38.710
as, you know, most of the performers

00:03:38.710 --> 00:03:41.280
I deal with are in their look.

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:44.200
You spend all this time in the
studio sculpting something,

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:47.240
sculpting a character, bring
it to set, you apply it,

00:03:47.240 --> 00:03:51.070
but it's not until it starts
to move as that character

00:03:51.070 --> 00:03:52.760
that it really comes to life.

00:03:52.760 --> 00:03:54.160
And I don't know if you know any

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:56.150
135-year-old people, but I don't.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:03:57.608
One hundred and thirty-five does give you

00:03:57.608 --> 00:03:58.900
a little bit of leeway.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:01.090
You know, it's like you can
push it a little bit there.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:03.420
- Yeah, he's like, "Prove
they don't do this."

00:04:03.420 --> 00:04:05.050
Whereas Joel had little to reference

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:06.360
when designing Charlie Manx,

00:04:06.360 --> 00:04:08.440
he had the opposite
challenge with another movie

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:11.170
he recently worked on, "Hellboy."

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:13.913
I got to try on a few
Hellboy accoutrements.

00:04:16.790 --> 00:04:18.610
New Hellboy, let's reboot it again.

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:19.443
I'm ready.

00:04:19.443 --> 00:04:20.276
Joel: Let's go.

00:04:21.980 --> 00:04:22.850
Narrator: Getting into this stuff

00:04:22.850 --> 00:04:24.753
is harder than it looks.

00:04:26.440 --> 00:04:29.631
- Age 100 was about three
to three and a half hours

00:04:29.631 --> 00:04:31.840
to get him all ready.
Yeah, I mean, it's long

00:04:31.840 --> 00:04:34.380
to get this done correctly

00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:39.080
because it is an old age,
and it is a realistic makeup.

00:04:39.080 --> 00:04:40.580
It took what it took,

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:42.540
and that was about three
to three and a half.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:47.120
"NOS4A2" trying to do four
stages of an age makeup

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:50.480
that looks absolutely
believable is very difficult.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.490
You know, that's a challenge,
that's a big challenge

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:54.640
trying to fool people in real life,

00:04:54.640 --> 00:04:56.280
and, you know, once you do that,

00:04:56.280 --> 00:04:58.480
you know you'll be able
to fool 'em on film.

